{"author":{"id":"673e1e1b6fccff4da5524407c8a8cdc7caf18221aabb93f2bb36ffef5c2a95e8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779765835,"nonce":"YMMkfxAHQqkI8PFdqIZJHsmomWQ=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOSn4-Gc8AAAABDXoYKw","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/karpenter-provider-rackspace-spot/issues/2","origin":"github"},"title":"Preemption webhook receiver: graceful drain on 6-min notice","message":"**Post-MVP item carved out of the approved plan.**\n\nRackspace Spot delivers preemption notices via a push webhook (configured per Cloudspace via `Cloudspace.spec.webhook` / Terraform's `preemption_webhook`). Eviction grace period is ~6 minutes. The spot-go-sdk does NOT expose an event stream; webhooks are the only signal.\n\nBuild a standalone HTTP receiver subsystem that:\n\n1. Receives Rackspace's preemption POST payload (schema TBD — confirm format with Rackspace docs/support).\n2. Identifies which NodeClaim(s) are affected.\n3. Triggers graceful disruption via Karpenter's standard mechanism (annotate / delete NodeClaim, let the disruption controller drain).\n4. Ships as part of the Helm chart with its own Service / Ingress.\n\nWithout this, spot evictions are hard — pods just vanish and reschedule the painful way.\n\nEstimate: 1-2 weeks (HTTP receiver, payload parsing, NodeClaim correlation, ingress wiring, tests).","files":null}]}